iterations.reply:
I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The `with` clause is this node's entire authority and grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`. A grant to a new outbound host would also send each customer's reply text to an external service, and that belongs in the graph definition, not in a note. The rest of the spec is implemented as written. The body also asserts the `ensures` clauses, so a violating confirmation traps rather than being returned.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // requires: len(text) <= 100000 holds of every input (host-enforced).
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // ensures: delivered == true, present(session_id).
        // Trap rather than return an output that violates the contract.
        assert!(confirmation.delivered, "ensures violated: delivered == true");
        assert!(
            !confirmation.session.is_empty(),
            "ensures violated: present(session_id)"
        );

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I didn't implement the `http-client` change because the node's `with` clause is its whole authority and currently lists only `response-channel`. If the platform team has approved `aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0`, the graph definition's `with` clause should be updated first. Then this node can be regenerated with that import. Sending the reply text to an external host should also be reviewed before that change.

